    "created_at": "2026-01-23T19:27:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-23T19:28:01Z",
    "body": "I love the idea of using an `<img>` as the video poster inside the video element. This also could have the added benefit of allowing things like object-fit and object-position to be separate from the video.\n\nMy use case is in my WordPress themes, I design it to allow the user to pick an image or video background. This would make it easier to support this as if a video file isn't picked, then it could fallback to an image, while still having all the features a regular image has that a poster currently cant.\n\n```\n<video style='object-position: center;'>\n   <source src='<?= $media->url ?>'></source> // Show video if picked\n   <img src='<?= $media->poster->url ?>' style='object-position: 0% 50%;' /> // Fallback image\n</video>\n```\n\nRight now I either do this by wrapping both in a container and making them both absolute positioned. But technically this has some drawbacks for assistive tech as it will still see both image and video as the image isn't removed, just behind the video, should both an video and poster be set.\n```\n<div style='position:relative;'>\n   <video style='object-position: center;position: absolute;inset: 0;'>\n      <source src='<?= $media->url ?>'></source>\n   </video>\n   <img src='<?= $media->poster->url ?>' style='object-position: 0% 50%;position: absolute;inset: 0;' />\n</div>\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
